Title:
PhantomCtx: New Approach to Activation Context Hijacking for EDR Evasion

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post introduces PhantomCtx, a tool and technique for abusing Windows SxS/Activation Context (ActCtx) DLL redirection to load an arbitrary DLL into many signed executables without needing a known “vulnerable” sideload target. It explains how the Windows loader consults activation context DLL redirection early in DLL resolution, and why hijacking `PEB.ActivationContextData` is a reliable process-wide vector. The author first describes the common approach (injecting a crafted ActCtx blob and overwriting the PEB pointer via `NtWriteVirtualMemory`) and shows how it is detected by Elastic XDR rules focused on cross-process memory writes into suspended processes. The key contribution is a stealthier implementation that avoids remote memory writes by unmapping the original read-only ActCtx region and remapping a malicious pagefile-backed section at the same address, leaving the PEB pointer unchanged. This enables IAT-import DLL redirection (e.g., redirecting `advapi32.dll`) while evading those specific EDR heuristics. It’s primarily useful for red teams/pentesters and offensive security researchers focused on user-mode tradecraft and loader design.

Technical Focus:
- Windows SxS (Side-by-Side) manifests, assemblies, and DLL redirection
- Activation Context internals (ToC, DLL redirection section `Id == 2`)
- PEB/TEB activation context pointers (`PEB.ActivationContextData`, `TEB.ActivationStack`)
- Suspended process manipulation and loader behavior during process initialization
- Section/object-based memory mapping (`NtCreateSection`, `NtMapViewOfSection`, `NtUnmapViewOfSection`)
- EDR evasion against cross-process memory write detections (Elastic XDR rule logic)

Use Cases:
- Load a payload DLL inside a signed target process without classic `WriteProcessMemory` injection patterns
- “DLL hijacking” via ActCtx redirection against broadly available binaries that import DLLs via IAT
- Tradecraft research: replacing read-only mapped regions using section remapping instead of patching/writing
- Evaluating/validating EDR detection logic around suspended-process injection and remote memory writes
- Building alternative user-mode loaders that minimize suspicious APIs and RWX allocations

Keywords:
PhantomCtx, Activation Context Hijacking, ActCtx, SxS, WinSxS, manifest, DLL redirection, DLL search order, IAT imports, PEB.ActivationContextData, TEB.ActivationStack, CreateProcessW suspended, NtReadVirtualMemory, NtWriteVirtualMemory, NtAllocateVirtualMemory, NtCreateSection, NtMapViewOfSection, NtUnmapViewOfSection, Elastic XDR detections, user-mode evasion, signed binary loading